  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  One more round of strong to severe storms this\nafternoon into tonight, and lingering low-topped convection \nwith a continuing heavy rain threat tonight - focused over our \nSW Zones.\n\nThe ring of fire pattern persists today as a stubborn ridge of\nhigh pressure persists across the Central and Southeast US. Pockets\nof energy riding along the northeast periphery of that ridge\nhave brought numerous rounds of storms to Pennsylvania over the\nlast 3 days, and at least one more episode is on tap for this\nafternoon and evening. \n\nHi resolution guidance and the latest SPC convective outlook\nhighlight southwest PA into the Ohio Valley as the most likely\ncorridor for severe weather and heavy/possibly excessive rain. \n\nAt least one round of strong to severe storms will race from \nnorthwest to southeast through western Pennsylvania this \nafternoon. Damaging winds will be the primary threat, but some \nsmall hail is also possible. \n\nLooking at the ML CAPE via the HRRR and a nearly east/west axis\nof 1000+ J/KG CAPE values lingers over Somerset and Bedford\nCounties right through tonight - until 12Z Sat. Hourly FFG\nvalues are locally only around 0.75 across Bedford County which\nis a concern as the area near and to the south of I-76 stands\nthe best chance at deeper convection and excessive rainfall\nrates. \n\nFor locations outside of the Laurel Highlands & Bedford County,\nthere could still be occasional showers and perhaps a few\nthunderstorms today. Weaker forcing means severe weather is\nunlikely and rainfall amounts should generally remain below a\nquarter of an inch. It will remain warm and muggy today with\nhighs in the mid 70s to mid 80s and uncomfortable dewpoints.\n\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry, less humid weather returns for Saturday and lasts \nthrough Labor Day Weekend.\n\nA cold front draped across the PA/NY border will begin its\nsouthward surge tonight, helped by a deepening upper low over \nNew England. This push of northeasterly winds at the surface\nwill usher in cooler and much less humid air for Saturday and\nthe remainder of Labor Day weekend. There could still be a few\nlingering showers in the southwest part of our area into\nSaturday morning, but coverage and intensity of storms will\ntrend considerably lower. With cool temperatures aloft, we can't\nrule out the chance for an isolated shower or two on Saturday\naround the area along with widespread stratocumulus clouds.\nSaturday will still be a few degrees warmer than average for\nthis time of year, but the real relief arrives by Saturday\nnight. Dewpoints and low temperatures will fall into the upper \n40s and 50s areawide by Sunday morning.\n \nOther than some morning valley fog, the weather will be really \nnice for the Sunday and Labor Day Monday. Highs mainly in the \n70s and lows 45-55F both Sunday night and Monday night will be\nideal for outdoor activities. Little if any rain is expected\nalong with abundant sunshine. At this point, it looks like dry\nweather sticks around through at least midweek. After that, the\nridge may build once again along with the associated chance for\na return to the ring of fire.",
